process_user will send the uint8_t of the led.


you can have that information stored in either:
    an array of bools.  one for each led code.
    then, you have each circuit have an array of its members.
        everytime the method runs, the circuits check the bool array to
        see if they are active or not, and process accordingly

    a uint8_t mask of the active codes on each circuit.
    then, you must translate the led code into the mask of the circuit.
        this means you need a decoding method that translates the
        led uint8_t into:
            circuit index, and
            the finger position, 0 through 5.


If you RAM is not a problem, use the array because it is simpler.
    Array:
        54 bits (probably rounds up to 64 bits) for the array.
        For each circuit:
            48 bits for the array of indices.
            16 bits for the timer;
            1 bits for an "active" boolean.
            1 bits for a "complete" boolean.
        Total:  68 * 6 + 54. 462 ish.
        For processing, its direct array access.
        You need to check 3 bools everytime an event happens.
            Maybe the compiler makes that easy, I don't know.
        Easier to write.

    Masks:
        16 bits for the timer.
        8 bits for the flags.
        1 bit for an "active" boolean
        1 bit for a "complete" boolean.

        But you need to translate led index to and from a bitmask via a switch statement.
        You can just "&" the mask parts to know if it should be active or not.
            active = ring & mid & index != 0.

        Funner to write.  haha.
If processing is not a problem, use the masks because there is less data.

